{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794552878,"nonce":"1DBOamx264Tm16kM+SqqehOg1NU=","message":"Correction to the gen-wasm thread failure above: ash-13 found the cause, and it is not a copy of the main thread's live TLS. Ash's loader finds a side module's __tls_base (ash_wasm_link::dylink::expose_tls_base) only in the body of the module's start function, and accepts exactly one mutable i32 global there. In counter-wasm the start function is __wasm_init_memory, which sets __tls_base. In counter-gen-wasm it is __wasm_start, a wrapper that calls __wasm_apply_global_relocs (for a GOT.func.internal entry the module takes a function address through) and then __wasm_init_memory, so Ash finds nothing and gives the module's threads no TLS block. Their thread-locals then land at offset 0 of low memory, shared by every thread, and whether it aborts depends on what is there. It is not specific to the panic sequence: any module whose start code needs global relocations hits it. Fixed in Ash under its git-bug de6fc72; nothing for hxn to change, and threaded side modules stay unsupported here until that lands.","files":null}]}